6.096424,"d":1481},{"n":"First Bank","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":18.443873,"lo":-66.116429,"d":2126},{"n":"Super One Foods","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":18.441816,"lo":-66.118208,"d":2405},{"n":"Oriental","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":18.441391,"lo":-66.118433,"d":2458},{"n":"Don Goyo Mini Market","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":18.439861,"lo":-66.117563,"d":2587},{"n":"Biblioteca Carnegie","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.468333,"lo":-66.109444,"d":706,"ds":"The Biblioteca Carnegie (or Carnegie Library) on Avenida Juan Ponce de León in Puerta de Tierra, San Juan, Puerto Rico is a NRHP-listed Carnegie library funded through a $100,000 donation from the Carnegie Foundation, becoming Puerto Rico's first purpose-built library when it was completed in 1915.","wpu":"Biblioteca Carnegie","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Biblioteca_Carnegie%2C_San_Juan%2C_Puerto_Rico._%2802%29.jpg/960px-Biblioteca_Carnegie%2C_San_Juan%2C_Puerto_Rico._%2802%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hotel El Convento","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.465838,"lo":-66.118228,"d":967,"ds":"Hotel El Convento is a small hotel in Old San Juan, Puerto Rico, in what was a Carmelite convent, adjoining the San Juan Cathedral square. The cathedral is the second oldest cathedral in the Western Hemisphere. Hotel El Convento is the oldest member of the Historic Hotels of America.","wpu":"Hotel El Convento","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/El_Convento_Hotel%2C_San_Juan.JPG/960px-El_Convento_Hotel%2C_San_Juan.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Arsenal de San Juan","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.466111,"lo":-66.101667,"d":990,"ds":"The former Spanish Navy Arsenal of San Juan (Spanish: Arsenal de la Marina Española), historically known as the San Juan Naval Station and also known as the Navy Arsenal of La Puntilla (Arsenal de la Marina de la Puntilla), the Arsenal of San Juan (Arsenal de San Juan) or simply El Arsenal, is a historic building complex and former Spanish Navy armory located in La Puntilla, a former neighborhood of the Old San Juan .","wpu":"Arsenal de San Juan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Old_San_Juan_-_Naval_Arsenal_entrance.jpg/960px-Old_San_Juan_-_Naval_Arsenal_entrance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"La Fortaleza","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.464167,"lo":-66.119167,"d":996,"ds":"La Fortaleza (English: \"the fortress\"), also known as the Palacio de Santa Catalina (\"Saint Catherine's Palace\"), is the official residence and workplace of the Governor of Puerto Rico. Located in the historic quarter of Old San Juan in the capital city and municipality of San Juan, it has housed the governor since the 16th century, making it the oldest executive seat in continuous use in the New World.","wpu":"La Fortaleza","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Fortaleza_in_Old_San_Juan_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Fortaleza_in_Old_San_Juan_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ballajá Barracks","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.465833,"lo":-66.119444,"d":1083,"ds":"The Ballajá Barracks (Cuartel de Ballajá in Spanish) is a historic building and former military barracks located in the Ballajá section of Old San Juan, in the city of San Juan, Puerto Rico. It is located close to El Morro and its esplanade, between the Morovis, Beneficiencia and Norzagaray streets. It was built from 1854 to 1864 to house Spanish and Puerto Rican regiments.","wpu":"Ballajá Barracks","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Museo_de_las_Americas_-_San_Juan%2C_Puerto_Rico_-_DSC06880.JPG/960px-Museo_de_las_Americas_-_San_Juan%2C_Puerto_Rico_-_DSC06880.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archdiocese of San Juan de Puerto Rico","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.4678,"lo":-66.1186,"d":1112,"ds":"The Archdiocese of San Juan de Puerto Rico (Latin: Archidiœcesis Sancti Joannis Portoricensis; Spanish: Arquidiócesis de San Juan de Puerto Rico) is a diocese of the Catholic Church comprising the northeast portion of the island of Puerto Rico.","wpu":"Archdiocese of San Juan de Puerto Rico","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Catedral_de_San_Juan_Bautista_a.jpg/960px-Catedral_de_San_Juan_Bautista_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"San Juan Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.452208,"lo":-66.114178,"d":1174,"ds":"San Juan Bay (Spanish: Bahía de San Juan) is a semi-enclosed bay, estuary, and harbor connected to the North Atlantic Ocean in the northeastern coastal plain of the main island of Puerto Rico.","wpu":"San Juan Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Vista_Aerea%2C_Al_Fondo_Bahia_de_San_Juan%2C_Toa_Alta_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Vista_Aerea%2C_Al_Fondo_Bahia_de_San_Juan%2C_Toa_Alta_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museo de las Américas","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.4677,"lo":-66.1201,"d":1239,"ds":"Museo de las Américas is a multidisciplinary museum in San Juan, Puerto Rico dedicated to the arts, folklore, architecture and broader history of the Americas. Its mission is to offer a synoptic view of the history and culture of the Americas from ancient to contemporary, emphasizing in Puerto Rico and its relationship to the continent, through exhibition programs and cultural activities.","wpu":"Museo de las Américas","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Ballaja_Barracks%2C_Museo_de_las_America%2C_San_Juan_PR.jpg/960px-Ballaja_Barracks%2C_Museo_de_las_America%2C_San_Juan_PR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Santa María Magdalena de Pazzis Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.469722,"lo":-66.120278,"d":1382,"ds":"The Santa María Magdalena de Pazzis Cemetery (Spanish: Cementerio Santa María Magdalena de Pazzis) is a historic Catholic cemetery located in Old San Juan, Puerto Rico. It is the final resting place of many of Puerto Rico's most prominent natives and residents. Construction began in 1863 under the auspices of Ignacio Mascaro.","wpu":"Santa María Magdalena de Pazzis Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Cementerio_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_Magdalena_de_Pazzi_proxima.jpg/960px-Cementerio_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_Magdalena_de_Pazzi_proxima.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.470833,"lo":-66.124167,"d":1788,"ds":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro (English: Promontory Castle of Saint Philip), most commonly known as El Morro (The Promontory), is a large fortress and citadel in the Old San Juan historic quarter of San Juan, the capital city and municipality of Puerto Rico.","wpu":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg/960px-Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Puerto Rico National Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.4638,"lo":-66.0923,"d":1877,"ds":"The Puerto Rico National Library (Spanish: Biblioteca Nacional de Puerto Rico) is the national library of Puerto Rico. It was created in 1967 as the Biblioteca General de Puerto Rico (Puerto Rico General Library) by Joint Resolution No. 44 of the Puerto Rico Legislature.","wpu":"Puerto Rico National Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Puerto_Rico_facade_-_San_Juan_Puerto_Rico.jpg/960px-Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Puerto_Rico_facade_-_San_Juan_Puerto_Rico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Luis Muñoz Rivera Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.464167,"lo":-66.090556,"d":2064,"ds":"The Luis Muñoz Rivera Park (or Parque Luis Muñoz Rivera in Spanish) is a 27.2 acre (110,000 m2) recreational public space located in Puerta de Tierra in San Juan, Puerto Rico. The park was named in honor of Puerto Rican statesman Luis Muñoz Rivera. It is the largest public square in the San Juan metropolitan area.","wpu":"Luis Muñoz Rivera Park","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Parque_Luis_Mu%C3%B1oz_Rivera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Templo del Maestro","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.462898,"lo":-66.089981,"d":2113,"ds":"Templo del Maestro (Spanish for 'Temple of the Teacher') is a historic Beaux Arts building from 1935 that originally served as the seat of the Puerto Rico Teachers Association (Spanish: Asociación de Maestros de Puerto Rico).","wpu":"Templo del Maestro","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Templo_del_Maestro_-_San_Juan_Puerto_Rico.jpg/960px-Templo_del_Maestro_-_San_Juan_Puerto_Rico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"El Escambrón Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.466944,"lo":-66.09,"d":2179,"ds":"El Escambrón Beach (Spanish: Balneario del Escambrón) is a public-access beach located in the San Juan Antiguo sub-district (subbarrio) of Puerta de Tierra, next to the Luis Muñoz Rivera Park in San Juan, Puerto Rico. The beach and recreational complex are located on the grounds of the former Escambrón Beach Club and Hotel which featured a large swimming pool enclosed by breakwaters.","wpu":"El Escambrón Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Escambron_Swimming_pool.jpg/960px-Escambron_Swimming_pool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Polvorín de Miraflores","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":18.449514,"lo":-66.093904,"d":2193,"ds":"The Polvorín de Miraflores is an 18th-century structure in San Juan, Puerto Rico, specifically in the Isla Grande (Big Island) sector of Santurce, that served as an ammunition storage place for the Spanish military. The site was included in the National Register of Historic Places in 1984 and on the Puerto Rico Register of Historic Sites and Zones in 2000 for its historic importance.","wpu":"Polvorín de Miraflores","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Polvorin_de_Miraflores_AC.JPG/960px-Polvorin_de_Miraflores_AC.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"San Juan ( san WHAHN, Spanish: [saŋ ˈxwan]; Spanish for \"Saint John\") is the capital city and most populous municipality in Puerto Rico, an unincorporated territory and insular area of the United States. San Juan was founded by Spanish colonists in 1521, who called it Ciudad de Puerto Rico (Spanish for \"Rich Port City\"). Puerto Rico's capital is the second oldest European-established capital city in the Americas, after Santo Domingo, in the Dominican Republic, founded in 1496, and is the oldest European-established city under United States sovereignty.","wpu":"San Juan, Puerto Rico","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/2013_Old_San_Juan_01.JPG/960px-2013_Old_San_Juan_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}